.TH NETECHO 1
.SH NAME
netecho \- announce an echo service, or dial one and say a word
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netecho announce
.I service
.PP
.B netecho dial
.I addr word
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Netecho
is the two halves of one TCP conversation, as two runs of one program.
A line crossing between two machines then needs nothing else on either.
.PP
.B Netecho announce
announces
.I service
on this machine, waits for one connection, and answers every line on it
with the same line until the far end hangs up.
It then hangs up its own end and exits, so it is started, used once,
and gone.
.I Service
is a port number, or a name
.B /lib/ndb/local
gives one.
.PP
.B Netecho dial
dials
.IR addr ,
a dial string such as
.BR tcp!two!echo ,
writes
.IR word ,
prints what comes back, and hangs up.
.PP
Both halves go through
.IR libnet 's
.IR dial ,
so the address is a name and
.B /net/cs
resolves it.
.SH EXAMPLES
On one machine of the bench, and then on the other:
.IP
.EX
% netecho announce echo &
% netecho dial tcp!one!echo crossing
crossing
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/netecho/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ping (1),
.IR net (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty, or names the step that failed:
.B usage
for a missing argument, or a line beginning
.B netecho:
saying what could not be done.
